---
import TVLayout from '../../layouts/TVLayout.astro'
import HomeBio from '../../components/HomeBio.astro'
import { SITE } from '../../config/site'
import { routes } from '../../i18n/routes'
import { useTranslations } from '../../i18n/translations'
import { absoluteUrl, websiteSchema, profilePageSchema } from '../../utils/seo'

const locale = 'en'
const t = useTranslations(locale)

const title = SITE.displayName
const socialTitle = `${SITE.displayName} — ${SITE.author}`
const description =
  'Olwtelet is the gaming handle of Isaac Rodrigues, a Computer Science student and full-stack developer in Brasília focused on applied AI and cybersecurity.'

const schemas = [
  websiteSchema(locale),
  profilePageSchema(locale, absoluteUrl(routes.home[locale])),
]
---

<TVLayout
  title={title}
  socialTitle={socialTitle}
  description={description}
  schemas={schemas}
>
  <HomeBio locale={locale}>
    <p class="big-text">
      Olwtelet is me, <span class="nowrap"
        ><button
          type="button"
          class="button-text bio__photo js-photo-toggle"
          aria-pressed="false"
          aria-label={t.bio.photoToggle}>Isaac Rodrigues</button
        >.</span
      > It's my gaming handle, and it ended up signing my work: two names, one signal.
      I'm a Computer Science student and full-stack developer in
      <span class="nowrap"
        ><button class="button-text js-channel-trigger" data-channel="8"
          >Brasília, Brazil</button
        >.</span
      >
    </p>
    <p class="big-text">
      A demo is like color bars: it proves there's a signal, not that the
      channel can carry the programming. The ideal scenario never goes on air.
      Below
      <a
        href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Channel_capacity"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">channel capacity</a
      >, a message arrives nearly error-free: not by silencing the noise, but by
      coding against it.
    </p>
    <p class="big-text">
      An attack is noise with intent. So security isn't the finish. It's the
      structure: what the system accepts, keeps and refuses. That's how I work
      at
      <a
        href="https://ordfall.vercel.app/"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">Ordfall</a
      >.
    </p>
    <p class="big-text">
      With AI, some of the noise is built in. If a simple rule works better, I
      stick with the rule. A model I can't test, observe and constrain is a bet,
      not a solution.
    </p>
    <p class="big-text">
      Away from code, I do another kind of programming:
      <a
        href="https://konect.gg/olwtelet"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">konect</a
      >
      <span class="nowrap"
        ><span class="bio__soon">coming soon to this channel</span>.</span
      >
      <span class="nowrap"
        ><button class="button-text js-channel-trigger" data-channel="1"
          >Tune in</button
        >:</span
      > explore <a href={routes.projects[locale]}>my work</a> and
      <a href={routes.certificates[locale]}>certificates</a>, visit my
      <a href={SITE.social.github} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >GitHub</a
      > or send me an <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>e-mail</a>.
    </p>
  </HomeBio>
</TVLayout>
